# This repository owns only the private FreeCodeGo extension. The official
# DeepSeek Harness source is downloaded into .upstream-cache/ during assembly
# and must never be added to this Git history.
/*

# Private workspace metadata and release documentation.
!/.gitignore

# The line-ending declaration this tree needs to keep its bytes stable across
# hosts. It is untracked without this line, and the 2026-09-19 restore lost it
# exactly that way: the worktree came back CRLF, and 13 load-bearing probe
# anchors stopped matching at once.
!/.gitattributes

# The Agent-Note tree stays unpublished, including its notes about this plugin.
# Exactly one note used to be whitelisted, which published a link target for
# nothing while the other ~540 notes stayed local; development history belongs in
# the review trail, not in the open-source repository's doc set.
!/README.md
# The front page ships as a pair: its language switcher points at the Chinese
# counterpart, so an unpublished counterpart would make the switcher a dead
# link on the repository's front page.
!/README.zh.md
# The consistency record ships with the pair, exactly as it does for every
# published package README, so the recorded state is readable from the
# repository instead of only from a maintainer's working copy.
!/README.i18n.yaml
!/COMPATIBILITY.md
# The community listing is a claim about this repository that someone else
# reads: `awesome-dsh-plugin` takes one YAML entry naming a subpackage URL and
# a pinned release tarball, and its maintainers check the description against
# the source. The file that records which values those are travels with the
# tree they describe -- each new Harness line refreshes one field of it, and
# that is an edit here rather than a fact remembered by whoever submitted it.
!/AWESOME_DSH_PLUGIN.md
# License and legal documents must ship with the open-source repository.
!/LICENSE
!/TRADEMARK.md
!/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
!/harness.config.json
!/harness.lock.json
!/package.json
!/pnpm-workspace.yaml
!/pnpm-lock.yaml
!/tsconfig.base.json
!/tsconfig.base.client.json
!/tsconfig.host.json
!/tsconfig.client.json
!/tsconfig.json
!/tsdown.config.ts

# The test and lint runners resolve their configuration from the working
# directory, and every `package.json` entry point below is invoked with
# `plugin/` as that directory, so these files have to be here or the entry
# point runs on defaults. `sync-harness` mirrors seven directories and the
# official packages; it does not restore root files, so a clone cannot get
# them from the assembly either. They are byte-identical to the pinned
# upstream commit, not local modifications.
#
# `vitest run` takes no `--config`, so without `vitest.config.ts` a clone runs
# the built-in defaults: no workspace-alias resolution and none of the
# `scripts/**/*.spec.ts` include this repository's own gate specs depend on --
# the very include the `release/freecodego-family.spec.ts` note below cites.
# Every other config here imports `vitest.shared.ts`, so that one is not
# optional beside them.
!/vitest.config.ts
!/vitest.shared.ts
!/vitest.e2e.config.ts
!/vitest.bench.config.ts
!/vitest.expected.config.ts
!/vitest.snapshot.config.ts
!/vitest.web.config.ts
!/vitest.web.perf.config.ts
!/vitest.web-stress.config.ts

# `lint` is `run-oxlint.ts .` and `verify-lint-budget` compares against a
# committed baseline; with no `.oxlintrc.json` a clone lints under oxlint's
# defaults, so the budget's per-rule counts stop describing anything. The
# staged variant is what the pre-commit entry point names.
!/.oxlintrc.json
!/.oxlintrc.staged.json

# `duplication` is `jscpd --config .jscpd.json packages scripts`.
!/.jscpd.json

# Editor-side declaration of the text conventions, and the declared pair of
# `.gitattributes` above: that file pins what git produces, this one what
# editors write to disk, which is the path git's filters cannot reach. A clone
# holding one without the other keeps half of the byte-stability story.
!/.editorconfig

# Deliberately not whitelisted, so a later reader does not re-add them by
# pattern-matching on the block above:
#
#   `lefthook.yml` -- `postinstall` installs lefthook and this file declares
#   the hooks, but lefthook resolves its configuration from the *Git root*,
#   which in this checkout is one level above `plugin/`. `lefthook dump`
#   returns `{}` from both directories, so the declared hooks are inert here
#   and carrying the file would not change that. Repairing it means placing
#   the configuration at the Git root, which is a decision about the outer
#   worktree rather than a whitelist entry -- and the hooks it would activate
#   run `run-oxlint.ts --fix` against staged files, which is not something to
#   switch on inside a checkout two writers share.
#
#   `pytest.ini`, `.rgignore`, `.gitlab-ci.yml` -- no entry point in this
#   repository consumes them: `run-gates.ts` declares no Python gate, no script
#   passes `--ignore-file`, and the published CI is the GitHub workflow rather
#   than the GitLab one.

# The upstream Harness doc tree stays unpublished. Two FreeCodeGo-owned
# documents used to be whitelisted here by name; both are gone from the tree, and
# a whitelist entry that names nothing is how a deleted document keeps looking
# published.
!/docs/
/docs/*

# Private FreeCodeGo packages only; all other packages are Harness core.
!/packages/
/packages/*
!/packages/freecodego/
!/packages/freecodego/**

# Dependency patches the ignored official Harness checkout declares in the
# published `pnpm-workspace.yaml` `patchedDependencies`. An install resolves
# those paths before anything is built, so a patch file left out of the
# repository makes a fresh clone uninstallable; all three are byte-identical to
# the pinned upstream commit rather than local modifications.
!/patches/
!/patches/*

# The script that fetches and assembles the ignored official Harness checkout.
!/scripts/
/scripts/*
!/scripts/sync-harness.mjs
# `!/scripts/check-upstream.mjs` used to sit here. That file is gone from the tree,
# so the entry named nothing — the same hazard the `docs/` block above describes.
# It was not redundant while it existed: it resolved `harness.config.json`'s
# `candidateRef` with `git ls-remote` and failed when the pinned candidate had
# moved. Nothing in the tree does that now — `sync-harness.mjs` reads the config
# only for `repository` — so recovering it is a decision, not a revert. Locate the
# deleting commit with
# `git log --diff-filter=D --format=%H -1 -- plugin/scripts/check-upstream.mjs`,
# then read its last content from that commit's first parent with
# `git show <that hash>^:plugin/scripts/check-upstream.mjs`. Re-add this entry
# together with the file. (Named by command rather than by literal hash on
# purpose: `verify-repository-references` rejects a maintained file that quotes a
# commit the local object database can resolve.)

# `pnpm run build:official` is the workflow's client-build step, and it is
# `tsx scripts/build.ts`. `sync-harness` does not restore `scripts/` at all, so a
# clone without this entry dies on a missing module at that step — the same
# "an omitted helper fails the published pipeline on its own imports" shape the
# helpers further down are whitelisted for. Its own imports
# (`client-build-environment`, `pnpm-invocation`) are whitelisted there too.
!/scripts/build.ts

# The published FreeCodeGo bundle's `prepack` runs this assembly script, and
# `release:pack` runs `pnpm pack`, so a clone that lacks it cannot build the
# tarball a release publishes. (`prepack` is npm's lifecycle, not npm's
# publication: the artifact is the release asset now.)
!/scripts/build-freecodego-bundle.mjs
# License compliance gates must ship with the repository so CI can enforce them.
# The in-package vendored-source spec ships with its gate: the disclosure table is
# generated from provenance records on disk, so a clone without this spec has no
# assertion that a copy cannot silently drop out of the notices.
!/scripts/gen-third-party-notices.ts
!/scripts/gen-third-party-notices.in-package-vendored.spec.ts
# Every FreeCodeGo package carries two TypeScript projects (build face and
# type-only test face), and `extends` does not inherit `references`, so the
# reference list is written twice per package. This spec is the only thing that
# notices when the copies drift; a clone without it silently loses the check, and
# the drift's own symptom points at `vendor/**` rather than at the config at
# fault.
!/scripts/freecodego-tsconfig-families.spec.ts
# The same reasoning applies to the two gates added beside it: a clone without
# `freecodego-config-readers` loses the only check that a Config field has a
# reader (the compiler cannot see it — the whole-object forward defeats the
# excess-property check), and without `freecodego-sync-overlay` nothing notices
# that the overlay below stopped shipping.
!/scripts/freecodego-config-readers.spec.ts
!/scripts/freecodego-sync-overlay.spec.ts
# And to the encoding gate. Its subject is a file that tooling cannot decode at
# all: oxlint answers such a file with `Failed to open file` instead of linting
# it, and the repository lint entry point turns that into a formatter panic
# (exit 127) when CI forces `--format=default`. One undecodable spec was the
# sole cause of that panic, and nothing failed while it sat there.
!/scripts/source-encoding.spec.ts
# And to the lint-workflow gate. Upstream's own contract spec pins the
# type-aware whole-tree `--fix`, which this fork cannot run — it deletes `!` and
# `as` assertions `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` requires. That spec is ignored
# (synced, not tracked), so the fork's divergence has to be pinned by a file a
# clone receives.
!/scripts/freecodego-lint-workflow.spec.ts
# And to the lint budget ratchet: a budget nobody can read is not a budget. The
# baseline is committed data, so it has to be tracked alongside the script that
# compares against it and the spec that proves the comparison reports growth.
!/scripts/lint-budget.ts
!/scripts/lint-budget.spec.ts
!/scripts/lint-budget.baseline.json
# The generator the gate above compares against. Without it the gate has
# nothing to compare to, and `build:freecodego`'s verify step cannot run.
!/scripts/gen-freecodego-test-tsconfig.ts
# The other two gates `build:freecodego` runs, both added with the
# 0.1.7-alpha.2 line. Each is named by path in the published `package.json`, so a
# clone without it dies at the workflow's build step rather than losing a check:
# the preset-patch generator is the module `--check` runs (`Cannot find module`),
# and vitest answers a spec path it cannot open with `No test files found,
# exiting with code 1` — a clone that lacks the spec fails the build, it does not
# silently skip the assertion. The public repository's 0.1.7-alpha.2 run died on
# the first of the two.
!/scripts/gen-preset-patches.mjs
!/scripts/freecodego-service-facades.spec.ts
# The free-model tables in the published READMEs are generated, and the markers
# around them name this script by path. Left out of the repository, the published
# front page would credit a file a clone does not have, and the two
# `generate-free-model-tables` / `verify-free-model-tables` entries in the
# published `package.json` would name a missing module — the same "an omitted
# helper fails on its own imports" shape as the entries above. Its spec ships
# with it: the region-replacement and rendering rules are what make a stale table
# detectable, and a check no clone can run is not a check.
!/scripts/generate-free-model-tables.ts
!/scripts/generate-free-model-tables.spec.ts
# `postinstall` names this file, and `package.json` is published whole — so a
# clone that lacks it cannot install at all, which is how the public
# repository's first release run died (`sync:harness` begins with an install,
# and pnpm reported `Cannot find module '…/scripts/install-lefthook.mjs'`).
# The file itself is published, not its subject: `lefthook.yml` and the pairing
# driver the hooks invoke stay private, so the installer skips a checkout that
# carries no hooks config (the behavior its spec pins).
!/scripts/install-lefthook.mjs
!/scripts/install-lefthook.spec.ts
# The private sync overlay itself. `sync:harness` replaces whole upstream
# package directories, tests included, so the specs covering the private seams
# live here as `.tpl` and are copied back on every sync. They were previously
# untracked — present only in one working copy — so a fresh clone could not run
# the sync at all. The templates are `.tpl` because the workspace tsconfig
# typechecks `scripts/**/*.ts` and their relative imports only resolve at the
# destination.
!/scripts/harness-overlay/
!/scripts/harness-overlay/**
!/scripts/verify-dsh-package-licenses.ts
!/scripts/verify-dsh-package-licenses.spec.ts
# The helpers `sync:harness` reads while it patched the upstream tree, and the
# sync runs in CI on every release: the published repository carries the fork's
# own packages only, so each release reconstructs the upstream workspace from the
# pinned Harness commit. These four were missing here, and release
# `0.1.6-alpha.2.2` died on the first of them —
#   ENOENT …/plugin/scripts/package-dependency-policy.ts
#   at patchHarnessV013Compatibility (scripts/sync-harness.mjs)
# — with every later step skipped and the publish job skipped behind them. The
# list is the whole set of helpers the sync reads directly outside `packages/`,
# taken from the script itself (`grep -o "join(root, '[^']*')"`), because adding
# them one failure at a time costs a release attempt each.
#
# Two of the four currently match upstream byte for byte, and they are named
# anyway: the sync's own `scripts/` pass is add-only, so a name this tree already
# has is left alone — which is what makes the fork's version win on the day one of
# them diverges, instead of upstream's copy silently replacing it. The other two
# diverge today (`package-dependency-policy.ts` carries the Client-face exclusion
# anchor the fork's patch asserts on).
!/scripts/package-dependency-policy.ts
!/scripts/session-fixture-layout.ts
!/scripts/session-fixture-layout.spec.ts
!/scripts/verify-package-dependencies.spec.ts
!/scripts/release/
/scripts/release/*
!/scripts/release/families.ts
!/scripts/release/families.spec.ts
# The fork's own release sequence, and the spec that pins its payload contract.
# `families.ts` gains the `freecodego` family here, so the divergence lives in a
# file a clone receives — but its spec did not, and neither did this one: the
# spec existed only in one working copy while `vitest.config.ts`'s
# `scripts/**/*.spec.ts` include kept collecting it there, so a clone ran one
# spec file fewer and the fork's release contract went unchecked. That is the
# same "present only in one working copy" defect `freecodego-sync-overlay.spec.ts`
# guards for the sync overlay.
!/scripts/release/freecodego-family.spec.ts
!/scripts/release/bump.ts
!/scripts/release/pack.ts
!/scripts/release/publish.ts
!/scripts/release/verify.ts
!/scripts/release/verify-packed-install.ts
!/scripts/release/verify-published.ts
!/scripts/release/process.ts
!/scripts/release/tarball.ts
# The release notes. The workflow reads them from the published changelog
# through this module, so a checkout without it could not publish a release at
# all — and the same call is the check that a version has notes before the build
# starts. Its spec ships with it: the heading boundary it pins is what keeps
# `0.1.6-alpha.2` from being answered with `0.1.6-alpha.2.1`'s section.
!/scripts/release/notes.ts
!/scripts/release/notes.spec.ts
# The diagnosis a refused upload prints about itself. `publish.ts` reaches it
# only on a failure, which is exactly when a module error would be worst: the
# import is resolved before the registry's own refusal is echoed, so a clone
# without this file would report a missing module instead of the reason the
# release could not publish. Its spec ships with it, because its branches are
# the difference between "the job never had an OIDC token" and "npm's side
# names something else", and a check no clone can run is not a check.
!/scripts/release/auth-diagnosis.ts
!/scripts/release/auth-diagnosis.spec.ts
# The registry reads the publish and verify steps share, including the wait for
# a version the registry acknowledged to become readable. A clone without it
# fails both steps on this module's import, which is the one failure the release
# pipeline cannot diagnose for itself. Its spec ships with it: the wait is what
# separates "the registry is slow" from "the publish never landed", and a wait
# no clone can run is a wait nobody has checked.
!/scripts/release/registry.ts
!/scripts/release/registry.spec.ts
# Helper modules the published scripts above import. The release workflow in
# `../.github/workflows/release-freecodego.yml` runs `release:verify`,
# `release:pack`, `release:verify-packed-install`, `release:publish`, and
# `release:verify-published`, and the notices generator
# resolves the browser-bundled inputs through `browser-bundled-externals`; an
# omitted helper fails the published pipeline on its own imports.
!/scripts/browser-bundled-externals.ts
!/scripts/client-build-environment.ts
!/scripts/pnpm-invocation.ts
!/scripts/publication-payload.ts
!/scripts/release/installed-product-isolation.ts
# `.github/` is deliberately absent from this allowlist, and the reason is a
# location rather than a choice: GitHub resolves workflows, issue and
# pull-request templates and Dependabot against `<repository root>/.github`
# only, so the copy that shipped here was published without ever being loaded.
# The release workflow was the expensive half of that — it is what creates the
# release asset the community listing's entry pins, so it was inert exactly
# where it mattered. The metadata now lives at the repository root, where the
# root `.gitignore` names it (`!/.github/`, `!/.github/**`) for the same reason
# this file once had to name it here.
#
# The upstream tree's CI policy notes, pull-request approval policy, preview
# sizing policy, and issue-management bot stay unpublished: they document or
# drive workflows this repository does not publish — and the issue bot's config
# names the upstream organization's Projects board — so publishing them would
# ship instructions for automation that is not here.

# Generated output and installed dependencies remain excluded even inside the
# explicitly included FreeCodeGo package tree. These rules follow the
# allowlist so they cannot be re-included by the FreeCodeGo directory rule.
**/node_modules/
**/node_modules.stale-*/
**/lib/
**/dist/
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.log

# One-shot scratch files. A `.tmp-` name means throwaway, but the tree beneath
# `packages/freecodego/` is included whole, so a scratch file that lands before
# anyone looks is committed by whoever next runs `git add -A`. Three did
# exactly that in the package root — a `harness-plugin/.tmp-r30-apply.py` patch
# script, its `.tmp-r30-tests.py` companion, and the `.tmp-r30-t1.txt` snippet
# they injected — all cited nowhere, none of them in the package's `files`
# list, and the code the snippet carried already in
# `src/media-utils.ts` under a test. The rule has to be written *here* rather
# than in the root `.gitignore`, which carries a rule for
# `harness-plugin/scripts/.tmp-*` written for the same defect one generation
# earlier: a pattern in the outer file is decided against by line 114's
# `!/packages/freecodego/**`, because a deeper `.gitignore` overrides a
# shallower one. `git check-ignore -v <scratch path>` reports line 114 and never
# the root rule, which is why that rule blocked none of the three.
**/.tmp-*

# Runtime payloads are verified release binaries. They are downloaded by the
# native runtime host and belong in release storage, not source Git history.
/packages/freecodego/runtime-*/artifacts/
/packages/freecodego/**/.downloads/

# The plugin's per-workspace data directory — `<workspace>/.freecodego/**` holds
# the repo-map cache, hook chains and skill drafts written while the plugin runs
# (`data-home.ts` keeps *machine* state under the harness home; this one is
# keyed to the workspace). The repo-map cache alone is megabytes, so a checkout
# that ran an eval with its cwd inside `packages/` would otherwise commit it —
# and, because `pnpm-workspace.yaml` matches `packages/*/*`, the stray directory
# also fails `check-workspace-constraints` with "expected a package here".
**/.freecodego/
